From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jay Leaton <58495321+jayleaton@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 9 Nov 2026 21:16:49 +0700
Subject: [PATCH 34/79] compute lane: S3 on the shared runner (generic, off by
 default)

A compute lane call whose code the runner's frontend accepts runs on the circuit; anything it declines runs in the
sandbox as before. Off unless the boot sets TF_COMPUTE_LANE_RUNNER=1 (DeepSeek-V4.1: or TF_DSV41_COMPUTE_LANE_RUNNER=1)
beside an enabled compute lane.

- compute_circuit.zig: the executor (Exec.run: a fresh python fence cell, a busy wait under _BUSY_MS, the _WALL_MS
  safety net, cancel on a gone client), its knobs (_WALL_MS, _BUSY_MS, _VERIFY, _ELF) and the decline reasons
  (`markup` among them). compute_circuit.binding puts S3's switch and knobs under a family's compute lane names;
  compute_circuit.open opens its runner, sharing the family runner's loaded circuit and bank when they name the
  same files; with the lane off it warns and stays off.
- Engine API: Info.compute_runner. Flash-Next's native CUDA host and DeepSeek-V4.1's serve engine open it from
  their compute lane bindings.
- Server: compute_exec runs built-in calls on the circuit first. Each report names its executor ("in-model runner"
  or "code tool"), call_ms, the program and steps or the decline reason; _VERIFY inserts the sandbox's result and
  reports same or differ. runtime compute counts runner_calls and tool_calls. With S3 off, replies are unchanged.
- Tests: compute_circuit_test (scripted frontend and backend; the knob names), compute_circuit_proof_test (recorded
  arm-d calls through the real frontend and CPU circuit print the sandbox's bytes; the circuit loads once beside
  the cell runner), the chat route with S3 in compute_lane_http_test (test-compute-lane-http-host
  now runs them). bench-runner-s3 measures lane calls through a served runner (TF_COMPUTE_LANE_RUNNER* knobs).

Ported from the dsv41 release line (s86 7b79ef92d).
